Morris inorder traversal follows the same sorted-order rule, but it avoids a stack. When a node has a left child, a temporary link is made from the rightmost node in that left subtree back to the current node.

The link is removed before moving on, so the tree has its original shape when the method finishes. The code also uses a `long` previous value so the first value does not need a special integer sentinel.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public bool IsBST(TreeNode root) {
        TreeNode current = root;
        TreeNode previousNode = null;
        long previousValue = 0;
        bool hasPrevious = false;

        while (current != null) {
            if (current.left == null) {
                if (hasPrevious && current.data <= previousValue) {
                    return false;
                }

                previousValue = current.data;
                hasPrevious = true;
                current = current.right;
            } else {
                TreeNode predecessor = current.left;

                while (predecessor.right != null && predecessor.right != current) {
                    predecessor = predecessor.right;
                }

                if (predecessor.right == null) {
                    predecessor.right = current;
                    current = current.left;
                } else {
                    predecessor.right = null;

                    if (hasPrevious && current.data <= previousValue) {
                        return false;
                    }

                    previousValue = current.data;
                    hasPrevious = true;
                    current = current.right;
                }
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

This changes a few pointers temporarily, but restores each one. A single-node tree and an empty tree both return true.

Time complexity: O(n).
Space complexity: O(1) extra space.

Inorder traversal of a valid BST produces values in strictly increasing order. This version visits nodes without recursion and compares each value with the one visited just before it.

A stack keeps the path to the next node. Duplicate values are rejected because the current value must be greater than the previous one.

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

class Solution {
    public boolean isBST(TreeNode root) {
        Deque<TreeNode> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        TreeNode current = root;
        long previous = Long.MIN_VALUE;
        boolean hasPrevious = false;

        while (current != null || !stack.isEmpty()) {
            while (current != null) {
                stack.push(current);
                current = current.left;
            }

            current = stack.pop();

            if (hasPrevious && current.data <= previous) {
                return false;
            }

            previous = current.data;
            hasPrevious = true;
            current = current.right;
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

For a tree with values 2, 1, and 3, the visit order is 1, 2, 3, so the check passes. An empty tree also returns true.

Time complexity: O(n).
Space complexity: O(h), with h being the tree height.

A node must stay inside the range allowed by all of its ancestors. For example, a node in a right subtree must be bigger than the parent, but it may also need to stay below an older ancestor.

The helper carries a lower and upper limit. A null tree is valid, and every real node must fit between those limits.

```cpp
#include <climits>

class Solution{
    bool check(TreeNode* node, long long low, long long high) {
        if (node == nullptr) {
            return true;
        }

        if (node->data <= low || node->data >= high) {
            return false;
        }

        return check(node->left, low, node->data) &&
               check(node->right, node->data, high);
    }

public:
    bool isBST(TreeNode* root){
        return check(root, LLONG_MIN, LLONG_MAX);
    }
};
```

Time complexity: O(n), since each node is checked once.
Space complexity: O(h) for the recursion stack, where h is the tree height.

This is the direct version: keep the values of the ancestors while walking down the tree. For each node, every smaller ancestor requires the node to be in its right side, and every larger ancestor requires it to be in its left side.

It is not the most space-efficient method, but the condition is easy to follow. The list is copied when moving to a child, which makes the code clear at the cost of extra work.

```python
class Solution:
    def isBST(self, root):
        def valid(node, lower_ancestors, upper_ancestors):
            if node is None:
                return True

            for value in lower_ancestors:
                if node.data <= value:
                    return False

            for value in upper_ancestors:
                if node.data >= value:
                    return False

            next_lower = lower_ancestors[:]
            next_upper = upper_ancestors[:]

            for value in next_upper:
                if node.data > value:
                    return False

            next_lower.append(node.data)
            next_upper.append(node.data)

            return valid(node.left, lower_ancestors, next_upper) and \
                   valid(node.right, next_lower, upper_ancestors)

        return valid(root, [], [])
```

The important edge case is a node that looks valid compared with its parent but breaks an older limit. For example, 12 cannot be in the left subtree of 10 even if it is below its immediate parent 15.

Time complexity: O(n^2) in a highly skewed tree because ancestor lists are copied and scanned.
Space complexity: O(n^2) in the worst case because of the stored lists and recursion.

