## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
$O(N \log N)$ on average, and $O(N^2)$ in the worst case. For every node, we potentially traverse its subtrees to find the maximum/minimum values. In a balanced tree, finding the extremes takes $O(\log N)$ time per node, leading to $O(N \log N)$ total time. However, in a heavily skewed tree, finding the extreme nodes takes $O(N)$ time for each of the $N$ nodes down the line, resulting in an $O(N^2)$ time complexity. *(Note: A more optimal approach passes upper and lower bounds down the recursion to achieve $O(N)$ time).*

**Space Complexity:**
$O(H)$, where $H$ is the height of the tree. This space is consumed by the call stack during the recursive calls to `isBST`. In the worst-case scenario (a skewed tree), the recursion depth will be $N$, requiring $O(N)$ space. In a perfectly balanced tree, it requires $O(\log N)$ space. The helper functions (`largestNodeTree` and `smallestNodeTree`) are iterative and use $O(1)$ auxiliary space.
